==== 8.1.0~dev ===

Incompatible changes:
 * Without <commandpipe> in the configuration file, or ~command_pipe for
   Ocsigen.Server.start, the server has no command pipe. It used to create one
   at the path given to ./configure --commandpipe (by default
   /var/run/ocsigenserver_command), which the generated configuration file now
   sets explicitly. A configuration file that relies on this default must now
   give the path, to keep the reload order, and reopen_logs for logrotate.
   Ocsigen.Config.get_command_pipe returns a string option, and
   Ocsigen_base.Config_static.command_pipe is removed.
 * The data directory is removed, and the installation no longer creates it.
   The server never used it itself: only Ocsipersist did, when its own <store>
   or <database> was missing, and it now requires them. <datadir> in the
   configuration file is ignored with a warning. ~datadir of
   Ocsigen.Server.start, Ocsigen.Config.get_datadir and set_datadir,
   Ocsigen_base.Config_static.datadir and ./configure --datadir are removed.
 * Without <logdir> (or <syslog>) in the configuration file, or ~logdir for
   Ocsigen.Server.start, there is no log directory: the logs go to the
   standard output and standard error, which suits systemd and statically
   linked programs. They used to go to the directory given to
   ./configure --logdir (with opam, in the switch), and the server did not
   start when it was missing or read-only. The generated configuration file
   sets <logdir> explicitly. An empty path means no log directory.
   Ocsigen.Config.get_logdir and Ocsigen.Messages.error_log_path return a
   string option, and Ocsigen_base.Config_static.logdir is removed.
 * Without <mimefile> in the configuration file, or ~mimefile for
   Ocsigen.Server.start, the server reads no mime.types file. It read the one
   that the installation put in its configuration directory, an old copy that
   is no longer installed, and logged an error when it was missing. The file
   is now read at the first lookup of a type, rather than for each <host>, so
   ~mimefile also applies to the hosts given to Ocsigen.Server.start. An empty path means no file.
   Ocsigen.Config.get_mimefile returns a string option, and
   Ocsigen_base.Config_static.mimefile is removed.
   Ocsigen_http.Charset_mime.default_mime_assoc no longer holds the file:
   find_mime looks it up after the rules, and set_mime_file sets it.
 * Silent mode (-s) and daemon mode (-d) are now refused when the logs go to
   the terminal: always in serve mode and with --reverse-proxy, and with a
   configuration file that gives neither <logdir> nor <syslog>, or
   Ocsigen.Server.start without ~logdir. These modes redirect the standard
   output and error to /dev/null, so nothing at all was logged, errors
   included. With -d, the server fails before it detaches.
 * <logdir> and <syslog> together in the configuration file, or ~logdir and
   ~syslog_facility:(Some _) for Ocsigen.Server.start, are now refused at
   start-up. The manual said they were mutually exclusive, but syslog was
   silently chosen. An empty <logdir> (no log directory) is still allowed.
 * Ocsigen.Messages.console is removed. It printed on the standard output,
   and said it did nothing in silent mode, which it never checked. The
   "Process N detached" message of daemon mode, its only use, now goes
   through Logs (source ocsigen:main).
 * Ocsigen.Response.to_response_expert takes the method of the request
   (~meth), to send no body in response to HEAD.
 * Ocsigen.Multipart.post_params takes a function (~on_file) that it calls
   with the name of each uploaded file it creates, so that the caller can
   remove it.
 * Ocsigen.Response.respond_file takes ?if_none_match as the list of the
   lines of the header field, which may be written on several lines.
 * The one-command modes (ocsigenserver DIR, --serve, --reverse-proxy) now
   listen on the loopback interface (127.0.0.1) by default, instead of on all
   the interfaces, and their startup banner shows the address. The new
   --address option (and the ?address parameter of Ocsigen.Server.serve and
   reverse_proxy) chooses another one, such as 0.0.0.0 in a container.
 * Staticmod no longer serves the files and directories whose name starts with
   a dot, such as .git/config, .env or .htpasswd, except under /.well-known/
   (RFC 8615), and directory listings no longer show them. This applies to
   serve mode too. The new hidedotfiles="false" attribute of <static> (or
   ~hide_dotfiles:false for Staticmod.run) serves them again.
 * User configuration files (Userconf) can no longer use <revproxy>, which
   let a user reach the services that only the server can reach, nor
   <allow-forward-for/> and <allow-forward-proto/>, which made the server
   trust headers that any client can send. Such a file now gets a 500, and
   the error log says which directive was refused (it used to report the
   refusals of Extendconfiguration as syntax errors).
 * Accesscontrol: <allow-forward-for/> takes as the client the last address of
   X-Forwarded-For, the one that the proxy added, instead of the first one,
   which the client can choose when the proxy keeps the header it sent (nginx
   with $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for). Behind a chain of proxies, the new
   proxies attribute (?proxies for Accesscontrol.allow_forward_for) gives the
   trusted ones, whose addresses are skipped from the right. The
   check-equal-ip attribute (?check_equal_ip) is removed: the server refuses
   it.
 * <maxrequestbodysize> is enforced again: reading more than this many bytes
   of a request body, file uploads included, gives a 413. It had no effect
   since the move to Cohttp. The default is 8000000 bytes (8 MB): if you
   raised <maxuploadfilesize> above it, raise <maxrequestbodysize> too. The
   limit also applies to the bodies that Revproxy forwards: raise it on a
   reverse proxy in front of a service that accepts larger bodies, with the
   new --max-request-body-size option for ocsigenserver --reverse-proxy.
 * A request is now answered 400 (Bad Request), and its connection closed,
   when it has no Host in HTTP/1.1, several Host header fields, or an
   invalid Host: anything but a host name, an IPv4 address or an IPv6
   address in brackets, with an optional port (at most 65535). A host name
   is made of letters, digits, "-", "." and "_": the other characters that
   RFC 3986 allows, such as "," or ";", are refused, as a proxy in front may
   read the name differently. So are percent-encoded host names, raw
   non-ASCII names (send the ASCII form of an internationalized name,
   xn--...), and an invalid or empty host in a request target in absolute
   form (http://host/...). Such requests used to be served, by the last
   Host, by the first <host> of the configuration, or by the virtual host of
   the decoded name.
 * Staticmod answers files and directory listings to GET and HEAD only.
   OPTIONS now gets 200 without content and "Allow: GET, HEAD, OPTIONS".
   Other methods, such as POST, TRACE or unknown ones, now get 405 (Method
   Not Allowed) with the same Allow header, instead of the file. Error pages
   (the code attribute) still answer any method.
 * An HTTP/1.0 request without Host now goes to a virtual host that accepts
   any name (hostfilter="*" or none), like a request for an unknown name.
   It used to go to the first <host> of the configuration, whatever its
   hostfilter. A configuration without such a catch-all host now answers
   these requests 404: add one if a load balancer checks the server with
   requests without Host.

New features:
 * Security: the server now bounds how long it waits for its clients, so that
   slow clients, or clients pretending to be slow, can no longer hold its
   connections for ever ("Slowloris", "R-U-Dead-Yet" and "Slow Read" attacks):
   - the client timeout (<timeout>, its alias <clienttimeout>, and the
     ~client_timeout argument of Ocsigen.Server.start), until now parsed but
     never applied, bounds in total the TLS handshake and the headers of a
     request, and each part of a request body or of a response the client
     reads;
   - new <mindatarate> option (~min_data_rate), 500 bytes per second by
     default: the minimum rate of a request body, and of a response the client
     reads, past a grace of one timeout;
   - new <keepalivetimeout> option (~keepalive_timeout), 75 seconds by default:
     a connection idle between two requests is closed. Behind a reverse proxy
     that keeps its connections to the server alive, the idle timeout of the
     proxy must be below this one.
   Producing a response is not bounded, so that long computations, streamed
   responses and Eliom's comet requests are not cut. A single ticker checks
   all the connections once a second; reads and writes cost no timer.
 * Security: when the server is full (<maxconnected>), it makes room for a new
   client by closing the oldest connection that its client is not using (idle,
   without the start of a request, or with a TLS handshake not finished), as
   nginx does. Clients can no longer keep others from connecting with
   connections they do not use.
 * New <maxconnectionsperaddress> option (~max_connections_per_address): the
   maximum number of simultaneous connections from a single client address
   (an IPv6 address counts for its /64 network). No limit by default.
 * TLS is handled by Ocsigen Server instead of Conduit: each TLS port has its
   own context, and a session ends with a single close_notify alert, without
   waiting for the client's, which a client could withhold to hold the
   connection.
 * New <maxrequestbodysizeinmemory> configuration option: sets the maximum size
   of a request body kept in memory while decoding it (for instance form
   parameters), which could previously be set only through the OCaml API.
   Accepts SI/binary size units, like <maxrequestbodysize>.
 * The default maximum size of a request body kept in memory is raised from
   8192 bytes to 1 MiB, so that urlencoded POST requests larger than 8 kB,
   such as Eliom RPCs with large parameters, no longer fail.
 * New <loglevel source="name" level="debug"/> configuration option: sets the
   level of a log source by its name, e.g. a debug trace of one extension or
   application only, or a source of a library linked with the server, which
   the -v, -vv and -vvv options do not reach. The levels are set once the
   configuration is loaded, when the sources of the applications exist.
 * The default <maxconnected> is raised from 350 to 10000. Ocsigen Server 8.0
   was the first to enforce it, and an Eliom application keeps a connection
   for each page in use, for its comet channel: 350 were too few.
 * At startup, the server raises its limit on open files to what
   <maxconnected> needs, as far as the hard limit allows (and 1024 if Lwt uses
   select), and lowers <maxconnected> to what this limit affords, with a
   warning if it was set. Each connection may use two files, and 100 are kept
   for the rest of the server. Beyond the limit, the server could not accept
   connections any more. This needs Lwt 6.1, to know which engine Lwt uses.
 * Ocsigen Server now depends on conf-libev (except on Windows), so that Lwt
   uses libev, which handles more than the 1024 files of select.
 * OCaml API: Ocsigen_base.Lib.with_lock runs a function with a mutex held,
   like Mutex.protect, which OCaml 4.14 does not have.
   Ocsigen.Messages.level_of_string parses a log level, and set_source_level
   sets the level of a log source by its name.
   Ocsigen.Server.exec_config_file reads the configuration file and starts
   the server, and reports an error in the file as the ocsigenserver command
   does.
 * The server warns about <filebuffersize>, <minthreads>, <maxthreads>,
   <disablepartialrequests/> and <respectpipeline/> in the configuration
   file, as it does for <servertimeout>: they are accepted but have no effect.
 * OCaml API: Ocsigen_http.Header.Entity_tag parses lists of entity tags, as
   in If-Match and If-None-Match, and compares them (weak and strong
   comparisons of RFC 9110).
 * OCaml API: Ocsigen_http.Header.Date.parse reads the three formats of HTTP
   dates.

Bug fixes:
 * In syslog mode, reopening the logs (the reopen_logs order of a log
   rotation) no longer opens a new socket to syslog each time, which leaked
   one file descriptor per rotation.
 * Ocsigen_base.Loader.loadfiles, used for <extension findlib-package="...">
   and by Eliom for <eliommodule findlib-package="...">: the hooks that set
   the configuration of the element now surround all the files of the
   package, not only the last one, so that the libraries of an application
   split into several packages see it. A file loaded with force is now
   remembered: asking for it again as a dependency of another package no
   longer fails with "module already loaded".
 * Ocsigen.Server.start with ~logdir and ~syslog_facility:None no longer
   fails with "Log directory requested, but not set": choosing no syslog
   kept no log directory either.
 * Security: a response to HEAD no longer carries the body of the response to
   GET. The client, or a reverse proxy that keeps its connections to the server
   alive, took that body for the start of the next response on the connection,
   which could then be another user's, and even a response forged in a file the
   server serves. Responses with a 1xx, 204 or 304 status no longer get a
   "Content-Length: 0" either. The access log gives 0 as the size of a response
   to HEAD.
 * Static files: If-Range now uses a strong comparison, as RFC 9110 requires.
   The weak ETag of a file no longer matches it, and the Last-Modified date
   matches only once the file has not changed for a second. A client could
   otherwise get a range of a different version of the file.
 * The framing of a response now follows its body. A chunked body sent with a
   Content-Range, or with a Content-Length set by the code that made it, went
   out with neither chunking nor its length, so the client could not find its
   end. A fixed-length body now always gets its own length as Content-Length,
   even when the response had another one, or only a Content-Range.
 * A program that starts Ocsigen Server as a library, and installs no Logs
   reporter, now sees on the terminal the errors that stop the server before
   it configures its logging. Logs dropped them, so the program just exited.
 * Staticmod: a <static regexp> that does not match the path of the request,
   or a <static code> that does not match its status, now lets the next
   instruction answer, instead of failing with a 500.
 * A request with "Connection: close", or an HTTP/1.0 request without
   "Connection: keep-alive", now gets a response with "Connection: close",
   after which the server closes the connection, as RFC 9112 requires, without
   serving the requests the client may have sent after it. The connection used
   to stay open, waiting for a request that would never come. The options of
   the Connection header are read as a list, whatever their case
   ("Keep-Alive", "keep-alive, TE"). Responses now always say whether the
   connection is kept alive, as cohttp does for the responses it writes
   itself.
 * Accesscontrol's <allow-forward-for/> no longer answers with an Internal
   Server Error when X-Forwarded-For holds a single address, as nginx sends
   it, or when its last address is not an IP address.
 * Revproxy:
   - the query string of the request is forwarded. It was sent as part of the
     path, with its "?" encoded as "%3F".
   - an https:// target is contacted over TLS. The scheme of the incoming
     request was used instead, so a request received over HTTP was sent in
     clear to the HTTPS port of the target.
   - X-Forwarded-Proto is the scheme of the request (http or https), as the
     target expects. It was the HTTP version (HTTP/1.1).
   - X-Forwarded-For holds the bare address of the client alone: the address
     the server listens on (such as 0.0.0.0) is no longer added at its end,
     nor the addresses of the proxies before the server (after
     <allow-forward-for/>), so that the target needs to trust only the server.
     "forwarded:" no longer precedes a client address that comes from a
     trusted proxy, and a client without an IP address (Unix socket) is
     "unknown".
   - with keephost, the Host header of the request is sent to the target.
     The server connected to that host instead, so a client could make it
     connect to any host.
   - the Host header sent to the target no longer carries the default port
     when the destination URL has none (such as example.org:443), which
     targets that compare it strictly rejected.
   - the headers meant for a single connection (RFC 9110, section 7.6.1) are
     no longer forwarded, in either direction: Connection, the headers it
     names, Keep-Alive, Proxy-Connection, TE and Upgrade. The response is sent
     in HTTP/1.1, whatever the version of the target. A target that closed
     its connection, or answered in HTTP/1.0, made the connection of the
     client close after each response.
   - a request whose body cannot be read, or sent to the target, no longer
     stops the server. cohttp raised the error outside of any promise. A body
     that the client sends too slowly, cut short or too large no longer gives
     a 502.
   - the connection to the target is closed when the client leaves before
     the end of the response. It stayed open.
 * An existing file at the command pipe path is now used only if it is a named
   pipe that belongs to the user running the server. Anything else is refused,
   with an error in the logs, and the server runs without a command pipe; until
   now, another local user could plant a file of commands there. The server's
   own pipe is replaced with a new one, with a warning, if it has group or
   other permissions (Ocsigen Server 7 created it with mode 0o660): a process
   that opened it before loses its access.
 * The one-command modes (ocsigenserver DIR, --serve, --reverse-proxy) no
   longer create a command pipe. It was placed at a predictable path in the
   temporary directory, where another local user could create it first and send
   orders to the server. These modes have no configuration file to reload and
   log to the terminal; the server is stopped with a signal.
 * Deflatemod no longer compresses partial responses (206), nor any response
   with a Content-Range. The compressed body did not match its Content-Range,
   which counts bytes of the uncompressed file, and went out with no length and
   no chunked framing. This affected byte-range requests for text files in the
   one-command serve mode, where compression is on by default. Responses with
   no body (1xx, 204, 205, 304) are left untouched too.
 * Deflatemod removes Accept-Ranges from the responses it compresses. Byte
   ranges are only served from the uncompressed file, so a client resuming a
   compressed download got bytes of the uncompressed one.
 * A response with a status code that Cohttp has no constructor for, such as
   421 relayed by Revproxy from its target, is now sent and logged. Writing
   its access log line failed, and the client got a 500 instead.
 * The ssl dependency now requires version 0.7.0 or later, which the TLS
   hardening of 8.0.0 needs (Ssl.set_min_protocol_version). Ocsigen Server
   8.0.0 did not build with older versions, although its opam file allowed
   them.
 * The ocsigenserver-compat library now brings the old names of the base
   library (Ocsigen_lib, ...) and of the HTTP layer (Ocsigen_header, ...) with
   it, so that code written for Ocsigen Server 7 only needs this one library
   in its dune file. They used to require ocsigenserver-compat.baselib and
   ocsigenserver-compat.http as well.
 * The -v, -vv and -vvv options set again the level of the log sources
   created after the command line is read, such as those of the extensions
   loaded from the configuration file, of Eliom or of the application, as
   their default level. Since 8.0.0 they only reached the Ocsigen Server
   sources that already existed. The other sources of the libraries linked
   with the server, such as Cohttp's, still keep their level.
 * A request body shorter than its Content-Length, because the client closed
   the connection in the middle of it, is no longer handled as if it were
   complete: reading it fails with Request.Truncated_body, and the request is
   answered with 400. cohttp takes such an end of connection for the end of
   the body, so that a raw body, or the last value of a URL-encoded form, was
   cut short in silence. Multipart bodies were already rejected, for lack of
   their final boundary. A chunked body cut short is not detected yet.
 * The "shutdown notimeout" order of the command pipe no longer raises an
   exception: as the server does not wait for the requests in progress yet, it
   exits at once, like "shutdown". A delay that is not a number is now
   reported. The exception also stopped the command pipe for good, so that
   the later orders were ignored.
 * An order of the command pipe that fails no longer stops the command pipe.
   The later orders, such as the reopen_logs of a log rotation, used to be
   ignored until the server was restarted.
 * The size options of the configuration file (<maxrequestbodysize>,
   <maxuploadfilesize>, <maxrequestbodysizeinmemory>) now refuse a negative
   size, or one too large to be represented, instead of taking it silently: a
   negative size made every request body fail. The setters of Config,
   Server.start, Server.host and Extendconfiguration.maxuploadfilesize raise
   Invalid_argument for a negative size.
 * Virtual hosts now match host names case-insensitively, and ignore a final
   dot in the host name ("example.org."), in the request as in hostfilter. A
   request for "EXAMPLE.ORG" used to miss the <host hostfilter="example.org">
   configuration, and its access restrictions, and could be served by another
   host.
 * The error responses of the server, such as 404, and those of
   Response.respond_error, now say that their body is text (Content-Type:
   text/plain; charset=utf-8).
 * A request with a control character other than a tab in a header field
   value, such as NUL, is now answered 400 (Bad Request) instead of being
   served.
 * CONNECT requests are now answered 501 (Not Implemented): the server is not
   a forward proxy. The "*" target is accepted with OPTIONS only, and
   OPTIONS * is answered by the server itself, without content. Such
   requests used to be served like requests for the root page.
 * Empty lines before a request line, as sent by clients that end a POST
   body with an extra line end, are now ignored: the method used to start
   with them, so that the request was not seen as a GET. A method that is
   not a token is answered 400 (Bad Request).
 * The log reporters that Ocsigen Server installs, and its writes to the
   access log, can now be used from several domains or threads at once. Each
   message is formatted with no lock held, then written whole, in one call,
   to each of its files or terminals, under a lock of that destination only.
   With OCaml 5, two domains logging at the same time could make Format raise
   "Unsynchronized access to formatter". Requires logs >= 0.10.0.
 * A log that cannot be written, on a full disk for instance, no longer makes
   requests fail with a 500: the line may be lost, and the error is logged once,
   until a write to that log succeeds again or the logs are reopened.
 * In syslog mode, each message is formatted once, for syslog and for
   stderr: the printers of a message no longer run twice.
 * The logs:name level order of the command pipe now sets the level of the
   existing log source of that name. It used to make a new source with that
   name, which nothing logged to, so the order had no effect. An unknown level
   is now reported instead of turning the source off.
 * A request body too large to be decoded in memory is now answered with
   413 (Request Entity Too Large) instead of 500, and a warning naming the
   relevant configuration options is logged.
 * A server listening on a Unix-domain socket, which has no port (typically
   behind a reverse proxy), no longer writes port 0 in the absolute URLs it
   builds, e.g. the redirection of a directory to its URL with a slash:
   Ocsigen.Extensions.get_port returns the default port instead.
 * The log reporters installed by Ocsigen Server, which send each message
   both to the log files and to the console (or to syslog and stderr), now
   call the [over] callback of Logs once per message instead of once per
   destination. This makes Logs.set_reporter_mutex usable with them: the
   second release of the reporter mutex made the server fail at startup.
 * Reopening the log files (reopen_logs command, for log rotation) no longer
   races with the domains or threads that log at the same time, which could
   get Sys_error "Bad file descriptor". The new files are opened before the
   old ones are closed: if one cannot be opened, the logs keep going to the
   old files, and no channel is left open.
 * Ocsigen Server calls Unix.localtime and Unix.gmtime one at a time, through
   Ocsigen_base.Lib.Date.localtime and gmtime. With OCaml 5, they return a
   buffer shared by the whole program, so the date of a log line or of an
   HTTP header could be wrong when two domains needed one at the same time.
   The date of the log lines is computed once per second.
 * Rewritemod works again. Since 8.0.0, a request that did not match the
   regexp of a <rewrite> got a 500 instead of going on to the next
   instruction. As a rewritten request starts again from the beginning, a
   request that matched got a 500 too, unless it matched again.
 * The files uploaded in multipart requests are removed again once the
   request is handled, as the manual says. Since the move to cohttp, they
   stayed in the upload directory for ever. A file that the application
   moved, to keep it, is left alone without a warning. If the application
   answers before the body is decoded, the files created after the answer
   are removed once it is (see Ocsigen.Request.on_post_data_decoded).
 * An empty <uploaddir></uploaddir> forbids uploads, as the manual says. In a
   host or a site, the server refused to start with it. In <server>, it gave
   an empty directory name, so the server tried to write the uploaded files
   in the root directory.
 * ocsigenserver --version prints the version again, instead of
   0000000000000000, a placeholder that the build no longer replaces.
 * Accesscontrol: after <allow-forward-for/>, the <ip> conditions test the
   address given by the proxy, without the port that some proxies add after
   it (192.0.2.1:4711, [2001:db8::1]:4711). Before, they matched no forwarded
   address.
 * Accesscontrol: <allow-forward-for/> reads all the X-Forwarded-For lines of
   a request as one list, and accepts tabs around the commas. It read only
   the last line, and an address next to a tab was not recognized.
 * Outputfilter.run (`Rewrite ...) replaces every match of its regexp in the
   value of the header, as <outputfilter> does in the configuration file, and
   as its documentation said. It only rewrote a value that the regexp matched
   as a whole.
 * The reload order reads and applies the configuration file once, instead of
   twice. The extensions configured the sites twice (Eliom loaded the modules
   of its sites twice), and warnings and errors were logged twice. When the
   file has an error, the server now logs a single error, "Config file reload
   failed: " and the error, instead of "Config file reloaded". It keeps its
   sites, and the settings of the tags of <server> such as <maxretries>, as
   they were: the settings before the error used to be applied. What the
   extensions did before the error, such as loading a new extension, is not
   undone. The error no longer starts with "Fatal - ", as the server keeps
   running.
 * A malformed multipart body gets a 400 Bad Request instead of a 500: a part
   without a Content-Disposition header or without a name in it, a part header
   line that is not a header, a body cut right after its first boundary, and a
   multipart Content-Type without a boundary. A boundary that is empty or
   longer than 70 characters, which RFC 2046 forbids, was accepted and is now
   refused the same way. The reason is logged at the info level.
 * The parameters of the Content-Disposition of a multipart part are matched
   whole: "name" no longer matches "filename". A part whose filename came
   first got its file name as name, and a part with only a filename is now
   refused with a 400 Bad Request. Values without quotes are read whole
   (only their middle was kept). Parameter names are read whatever their
   case, with spaces allowed around "=" and ";", as RFC 7578 and RFC 6266
   allow. A malformed Content-Disposition, such as one with an unterminated
   quote, gets a 400 too.
   In a quoted value, \" and \\ are read as escapes, as some clients (Go,
   curl --form-escape) send them; other backslashes are kept. Browsers do not
   escape backslashes: when the parameters cannot be read with escapes, as
   when a file name ends with a backslash, they are read again with every
   backslash kept. Two backslashes in a row that a browser sends are read as
   one, as nothing tells them from an escaped backslash.
 * The boundary of a multipart Content-Type may be quoted, as in
   boundary="a b", and the names of the parameters of a Content-Type are
   read whatever their case, as RFC 2045 allows. Such bodies got a 400 Bad
   Request. Ocsigen.Request.content_type and the Content-Type of uploaded
   files now give the names of the parameters in lowercase, and their values
   without quotes.
 * When closing an uploaded file fails, the server no longer closes it a
   second time, which could close another file, and reports the first error.
 * A configuration file that is not well-formed XML is reported again, with
   the name of the file and the position of the error, and the server exits
   with code 50, as for the other errors in the file. Since xml-light 2.5,
   which is now required, the server stopped at start-up with an uncaught
   exception, and the reload order logged "Uncaught exception". A missing
   configuration file, or one that its DTD rejects, is reported the same way.
   A file of an <extconf> directory with such an error is still ignored, but
   its error is now logged with its position.
   Userconf: a user configuration file that is not well-formed XML is
   reported the same way, and a missing one lets the next instruction answer
   again, instead of a 500.
 * The warning for an optional extension that serve mode or --reverse-proxy
   cannot load no longer starts with "Fatal - ". At start-up, an error that an
   extension describes itself (Eliom, for instance) now starts with
   "Fatal - ", as the other errors that stop the server.
 * <port>80</port> listens on IPv6 again, as well as IPv4, as the manual says.
   Since 3.0.0, it listened on IPv4 only, while <port>[::]:80</port> also
   accepted IPv4 connections where the system allows it (Linux). The server
   now opens one socket for each family, and its IPv6 sockets accept IPv6
   connections only. Without IPv6, a port alone listens on IPv4 only. Each
   listening socket, not each port, counts as one connection in
   <maxconnected> while it waits for a new one.
 * Revproxy answers 502 Bad Gateway, instead of 500, when it gets no response
   from its target (refused connection, unknown name, TLS failure, connection
   closed). The error log gives the target server and the reason.
 * Errors that the client only sees as a generic status are now logged above
   the debug level: an internal error (500) as an error, a file the server
   cannot read (403) as a warning, and a bad or too long request as
   information. Since error responses no longer carry the exception (8.0.0),
   a 500 or a 403 could leave no trace in the logs.
 * Staticmod uses <mimefile>, and the <contenttype> and <charset> rules of
   Extendconfiguration, again. Since 3.0.0, they were read but not used:
   the Content-Type of a file came from the built-in table of magic-mime,
   without a charset. That table now comes after the rules and the file. Text
   and XML files get the charset of the host again, so a text file served
   from a configuration file now has "; charset=utf-8" by default (serve mode
   gives no charset, as before).
 * Static files: If-None-Match uses the weak comparison, as RFC 9110 requires:
   "x" now matches the ETag W/"x". All the If-None-Match lines of a request
   are read, and a comma within an entity tag no longer splits it.
 * Static files: If-Modified-Since gives 304 when the file has not changed
   since the date, not only when the date is exactly its Last-Modified one,
   and the obsolete date formats (RFC 850 and asctime) are accepted. The
   field is ignored when it is written on several lines (RFC 9110 13.1.3):
   the last line was used.
 * Static files: a false If-Match or If-Unmodified-Since condition is
   answered with 412, as RFC 9110 requires; they were ignored. As the ETag
   is weak, If-Match only succeeds with "*". Ocsigen.Response.respond_file
   takes ~if_match and ~if_unmodified_since.
 * Staticmod: the conditional headers and Range of the request no longer
   apply to an error page (<static code>): it could be answered with an
   empty 404 instead of a 304, or with a part of it.
 * Static files: a HEAD request with a Range header gets 200, as RFC 9110
   requires: ranges are only defined for GET. It got 206.
 * Deflatemod: content codings are case-insensitive, as RFC 9110 says:
   "Accept-Encoding: GZIP" now gets gzip.
 * Deflatemod: a response of a type that it compresses carries
   "Vary: Accept-Encoding" even when it is not compressed, so that a shared
   cache does not serve the uncompressed copy to the clients that accept a
   compressed one.
 * Deflatemod: 304 responses, and 206 responses of a type it compresses,
   carry "Vary: Accept-Encoding", as the complete response would (RFC 9110
   15.4.5 and 15.3.7).

==== 8.0.0 (2026-09-24) ===

Incompatible changes:
 * Module namespace modernization
   - Public modules are now grouped under three wrappers:
     * ocsigenserver (main library): Ocsigen.Server, Ocsigen.Config,
       Ocsigen.Extensions, Ocsigen.Request, Ocsigen.Response,
       Ocsigen.Messages, ... (replaces Ocsigen_server,
       Ocsigen_config, Ocsigen_extensions, Ocsigen_request,
       Ocsigen_response, Ocsigen_messages, ...)
     * ocsigenserver.baselib: Ocsigen_base.Lib, Ocsigen_base.Cache,
       Ocsigen_base.Loader, Ocsigen_base.Config_static (replaces
       Ocsigen_lib, Ocsigen_cache, Ocsigen_loader, Ocsigen_config_static)
     * ocsigenserver.http: Ocsigen_http.Header,
       Ocsigen_http.Charset_mime (replaces Ocsigen_header,
       Ocsigen_charset_mime)
   - Two modules keep their prefix inside the wrappers, to avoid name
     clashes: Ocsigen.Ocsigen_cohttp (with Cohttp) and
     Ocsigen_base.Ocsigen_stream (with Stdlib.Stream).
   - The migration guide (doc/migration.mld) lists all the renamings.
 * New ocsigenserver-compat opam package: provides the legacy
   Ocsigen_xxx module names as include-aliases of the new wrapped
   modules. Adding the ocsigenserver-compat, ocsigenserver-compat.baselib
   and ocsigenserver-compat.http libraries to a dune file keeps code that
   uses the old names compiling, apart from the base library helpers
   removed below.
 * OCaml 4.14 or later is required.
 * Base library: the helpers that duplicate the standard library are removed
   from Ocsigen_lib_base, and so from Ocsigen_lib (now Ocsigen_base.Lib),
   which includes it: the Option module (use Stdlib.Option), id (use Fun.id),
   the leftright type (use Either.t), List.map_filter (use List.filter_map),
   and the |> and @@ operators (those of Stdlib are the same).
   ocsigenserver-compat does not restore them.
 * Logging: the -v, -vv and -vvv options now set the level of the log
   sources whose name starts with "ocsigen:" and that exist when the command
   line is read, instead of the level of all the sources, existing or created
   later. The sources of other libraries, such as Cohttp, are no longer
   changed. Sources created later, such as those of the extensions loaded
   from the configuration file, of Eliom or of the applications, stay at the
   Warning level.
 * Dependencies: the compression backend no longer uses camlzip. The package
   now depends on bytesrw, conf-zlib and conf-zstd instead.
 * Access log: requests are now logged in the standard Apache/nginx Combined
   Log Format, understood by common log analysers, instead of the previous
   custom format. The line now reports the final status code, the response
   size and the authenticated user. Anything parsing the old format must be
   updated.
 * Logging: a server built with no default log directory, such as a
   development build, now logs to the standard output and standard error when
   the configuration file gives no <logdir> (and no <syslog>), instead of
   creating log files in the current directory. An installed server (opam)
   still uses its default log directory.

New features:
 * One-command serve mode: "ocsigenserver ./public" (or
   "ocsigenserver --serve ./public --port 8000 --directory-listing") now
   serves a directory of static files without any configuration file or
   pre-created log directory. The Staticmod extension is loaded on demand.
   Serve mode logs a startup banner with the URL it listens on, adds the
   safe-by-default security headers, and compresses responses.
 * One-command reverse proxy: "ocsigenserver --reverse-proxy URL" forwards
   every request to the base URL given, with no configuration file. The
   Revproxy extension is loaded on demand.
 * New extension Securityheaders (ocsigenserver.ext.securityheaders): adds
   common security headers with one declaration. Safe by default
   (X-Content-Type-Options, X-Frame-Options, Referrer-Policy);
   Strict-Transport-Security and Content-Security-Policy are opt-in. It is
   enabled by default in the sample configuration file and in serve mode.
 * Compression: the Deflatemod extension now supports the zstd
   content-encoding (RFC 8878) in addition to gzip and deflate.
 * Static files: serve an ETag and a Last-Modified header, and answer
   conditional requests (If-None-Match / If-Modified-Since) with 304 Not
   Modified (RFC 7232).
 * Static files: support single byte-range requests. Responses advertise
   Accept-Ranges, a satisfiable Range yields 206 Partial Content with a
   Content-Range header (honouring If-Range), and an unsatisfiable range gives
   416 (RFC 7233). This enables media streaming and resumable downloads.
 * OCaml API: Ocsigen.Server.serve and Ocsigen.Server.reverse_proxy start the
   one-command modes. Also new: Ocsigen.Config.set_global_log_level,
   set_log_to_stderr and get_log_to_stderr, Ocsigen.Messages.stdio_reporter,
   Ocsigen.Request.remote_user, set_remote_user and client_ip_to_string,
   Ocsigen_http.Header.Name.vary, Ocsigen_base.Lib.Url.split_decoded_path and
   Ocsigen_base.Lib.Date.name_of_month.
 * Documentation: the manual now lives in the main branch as odoc pages
   (doc/*.mld), compiled in place by `dune build @doc`, replacing the
   wikicreole manual of the wikidoc branch. doc/index.mld is the package
   landing page (manual table of contents), doc/api.mld the API reference
   and doc/migration.mld the migration guide to 8.0.

Security:
 * The <maxconnected> limit (maximum number of simultaneous connections) is
   now enforced again. It throttles the accept loop instead of being silently
   ignored.
 * Error responses no longer include the raw OCaml exception in their body.
   Clients now receive the generic HTTP status reason phrase; the exception
   details remain in the logs.
 * The command pipe is now created with mode 0o600 instead of 0o660, so that
   local users in the server's group can no longer send it control commands
   (shutdown, reload, ...).
 * TLS: the HTTPS server now requires TLS 1.2 at minimum (TLS 1.0 and 1.1 are
   deprecated) and prefers the server's cipher order. The <ciphers>, <dhfile>
   and <curve> entries of <ssl>, previously parsed but ignored, are now
   applied to the TLS context.

Bug fixes:
 * Request paths are percent-decoded again, as they were before the
   migration to Cohttp: Ocsigen.Request.path, sub_path and
   original_full_path used to return their segments still encoded,
   whereas the GET parameters were decoded. A file whose name is not
   made of unreserved characters can be served again, and Eliom
   suffix parameters get decoded values. Encoded ".." and encoded
   slashes cannot be used to walk up the tree.
 * Fix Ocsigen_base.Lib.Url.decode, which was silently returning its
   argument unchanged: its regexp was still written in Str syntax
   ('\|' for the alternation) after the migration to Re.Pcre.
 * Compression: a compressed response kept the content-length of the
   uncompressed body, which truncated it, and carried an entity tag that was
   not a valid one. It now drops the stale content-length and announces
   Vary: Accept-Encoding.
 * An unknown command-line option is now reported with a plain error message,
   instead of an uncaught exception.

